    {
      "id": "v2/5nn4_SC2_A_1017__top2/r00/bootstrap",
      "evidence_sha256": "b1b4ae3f1f273092f3c51fb7eb857ed7a48cf4e1844633cf76207404c3d1c567",
      "assessment": "Bootstrap retrieves interval delivery, endpoint geometry and population facts, then selects a reciprocal early probe, late increase and equal-duration mid-window transfer. Zero clipping and small delivered motion are correctly separated. However memory calls Best 7.708 about 0.30 below teacher 7.412; the actual inequality is reversed. Treating this as a ceiling and restricting potential gains to the middle is unsupported.",
      "decision_update": "R1 executes all four proposed arrays; the early increase improves Mean by 0.054475, reduction loses 0.004697, late increase loses 0.001437 and transfer loses 0.020347. The useful next question is marginal response and component attribution, not a teacher-score ceiling. H1 falsifier should distinguish delivery failure from delivered control with no affinity benefit.",
      "counterexample_or_limitation": "The mid-window transfer has equal duration and nominal dose, but equal nominal dose does not guarantee equal effective motion or isolate interval causality. Bootstrap has no response evidence; shared starts are not independent replications.",
